bta
To make Steam and Epic Games run smoothly on Batocera without users having to touch the Linux terminal or keyboard, you need to set up Flatpak integration and automated game scraping in your Master Image.
Here is the exact technical checklist to configure your Master Image before cloning it to customer SSDs:
1. Partition & Storage Setup (Crucial First Step)
Expand the SHARE Partition. By default, Batocera uses a small partition and leaves the rest of the SSD unallocated until the first boot. Boot your prototype SSD once on a PC so it automatically expands the SHARE partition to the full 500GB or 1TB capacity.
Keep SHARE as ext4 or btrfs. Do not reformat the user data partition to NTFS or exFAT. Steam Proton and Epic Games (via Wine) require Linux file permissions (ext4/btrfs) to launch Windows .exe files without crashing.
2. Steam Setup & Proton Tweaks
Install via Flatpak. Open Batocera’s terminal (F1 -> Applications -> Xterm) or connect via SSH and install the official Steam Flatpak (flatpak install flathub com.valvesoftware.Steam).
Enable Steam Play Globally. Open Steam in Big Picture Mode, go to Settings > Compatibility, and toggle on Enable Steam Play for all other titles. Set the default runner to Proton Experimental so Windows games like GTA 5 install and run automatically.
Fix Controller Focus. In Steam Big Picture settings, enable Steam Input for Xbox, PlayStation, and Generic controllers so users can navigate menus seamlessly after launching Steam from Batocera.
Log Out Before Cloning. Always sign out of your test Steam account and clear your login cache before creating your Master Image so buyers get a clean login screen.
3. Epic Games Setup (Heroic Games Launcher)
Install Heroic Flatpak. Run flatpak install flathub com.heroicgameslauncher.hgl in Xterm to add Epic Games and GOG support.
Pre-Configure Wine/Proton Path. Open Heroic once, go to Settings > Wine Manager, and download the latest Wine-GE or Proton-GE build. Set this as the default runner in Heroic's global settings so users do not have to download compatibility layers themselves.
Enable Auto-Add to Batocera. In Heroic settings, check the option to automatically create desktop/launcher shortcuts or use Batocera’s built-in Heroic integration so downloaded Epic games appear directly inside the Batocera console menu.
4. Console-Like UI & Auto-Scraping
Add Ports Menu Shortcuts. Place .sh launch scripts for Steam Big Picture (flatpak run com.valvesoftware.Steam -bigpicture) and Heroic inside /userdata/roms/ports/ so both storefronts sit right on the main Batocera home screen alongside retro consoles.
Enable Pad-to-Keyboard Mapping. Configure Batocera's Pad2Key profiles for Steam and Heroic so users can close a frozen game or exit back to the Batocera menu using Hotkey + Start on their controller.
Install a Clean Console Theme. Download a modern, console-style theme (like Art Book Next or Carbon) via Updates & Downloads > Themes and set it as default so the UI feels like a PS5 or Steam Deck.
5. Driver & Performance Tweaks (batocera.conf)
Enable Hybrid GPU Support. Uncomment nvidia-driver=true and nvidia-prime=true inside /userdata/system/batocera.conf. This ensures laptops with both Intel/AMD integrated graphics and an NVIDIA GPU automatically use the dedicated graphics card for games like GTA 5.
Enable Wi-Fi & Bluetooth by Default. Make sure Wi-Fi and Bluetooth services are toggled on in System Settings so buyers can connect to their home internet and wireless controllers right after booting.
