aur baki security and aisa kuch jisse baar baaar user ko boot na krna pde auto boot pe chla jaye mtlb plug krte hi khud hi booot ho kr xplay khule
Auto-booting directly on plug-in is impossible on a closed PC/laptop out of the box—for security reasons, Windows/BIOS will never automatically boot from a random USB drive until the user changes one setting in the BIOS once.
However, once they do a 1-time 30-second BIOS setup, every time they plug in your XPlay SSD and press the power button, it will automatically boot into XPlay—and when they unplug it, their laptop boots into Windows as usual!
1. How to Make It "Auto-Boot" (1-Time Setup for User)
Include a super clear 3-step guide in your box so the user sets this up once:
Step 1: Disable Secure Boot. Enter BIOS (F2, F10, or Del during startup), find Secure Boot, and set it to Disabled. (Most modern laptops block external Linux drives until this is off).
Step 2: Set USB Hard Drive as Boot Priority #1. In the BIOS Boot Order menu, move USB Hard Drive / UEFI USB above Windows Boot Manager.
Step 3: Save & Exit (F10). Now, whenever the XPlay SSD is plugged in before turning on the laptop, XPlay boots automatically. When the SSD is unplugged, Windows boots normally without touching any setting again.
2. Securing Your XPlay OS (So Users Don't Break It)
To stop buyers from accidentally deleting system files, changing critical settings, or copying your custom UI setup, lock down Batocera before cloning:
Enable Kiosk Mode in UI. Go to System Settings > User Interface Mode and switch from Full to Kiosk (or Kid). This hides dangerous system menus, file managers, and theme settings behind a hidden button combination (Up, Up, Down, Down, Left, Right, Left, Right, B, A) so buyers only see games, Steam, Epic, and basic Wi-Fi/Bluetooth settings.
Change Default Root Password. By default, every Batocera drive has the root password linux. Open terminal (F1 > Xterm) and type passwd to set a strong custom password so random users can't easily SSH into your OS partition and rip your custom scripts or theme.
Disable Unnecessary Network Shares (Optional). In /userdata/system/batocera.conf, you can disable public Samba file sharing (system.samba.enabled=0) if you don't want users browsing internal folders over Wi-Fi—though keeping roms accessible might be helpful if they want to add their own games.
Hide the Boot Partition on Windows. Make sure the BATOCERA boot partition does not have a Windows drive letter assigned or clearly label it XPLAY_SYSTEM (DO NOT FORMAT). Otherwise, when a user plugs the SSD into Windows, Windows pops up a "You need to format the disk before you can use it" warning for the Linux ext4 partition—which is the #1 reason non-tech buyers accidentally wipe external Linux drives!
3. Pro Tip to Prevent the Windows "Format Disk" Pop-Up
Add a Warning Sticker & Guide Note: Tell buyers explicitly: "Do NOT plug XPlay into an already running Windows PC—always plug it in while the laptop is turned OFF, then press Power. If Windows ever asks to format the drive, click CANCEL."
Create a Small exFAT "Transfer" Partition (Advanced Trick): If you partition the SSD with a small 20GB exFAT partition at the front labeled XPLAY_GUIDE containing your PDF manual and setup videos, Windows will open that clean folder smoothly instead of panicking over the Linux partition!
