This is the most important business question you could ask—and honestly, if you only pitch it as "a drive to download Steam games," most PC users will indeed just install Steam on Windows for free.
To make people happily pay ₹10,000–₹13,000 for XPlay, you cannot sell it as "storage for Steam." You have to sell what Windows cannot give them easily:
1. Why a Regular User Would Still Buy XPlay
Storage Expansion + Portability (The Core Value): Most Indian students and gamers buy laptops with 512GB SSDs. After Windows, college/office files, and software, they barely have 150GB left—which gets filled by just GTA 5 (110GB) + one more game. Buying a good 1TB external NVMe SSD alone costs ₹6,500–₹8,000. For ₹3,000–₹4,000 extra, they get that fast storage plus a complete gaming OS so they can carry their entire game library to a friend's house, hostel, or cyber cafe and plug it into any PC without redownloading 100GB.
The "Console on TV" Living Room Experience: Navigating Windows on a living room TV with a wireless controller is painful (pop-ups, mouse cursor, Windows updates, alt-tabbing). Plugging a laptop into a TV via HDMI and booting XPlay turns it into a PS5/Steam Deck-style living room console controlled 100% by a gamepad from the sofa.
Zero Setup Retro Library (The Real Hook): Setting up PS2, PSP, GameCube, Wii, and Nintendo Switch/Arcade emulators on Windows with proper shaders, BIOS files, box art, and controller mappings takes 10–15 hours of technical tweaking. If your drive comes pre-configured for retro emulation (with legal homebrew/freeware or ready-to-drop ROM folders) alongside Steam/Epic, casual gamers pay purely to save that headache.
Work/Study Laptop Separation: Many people use a parent's laptop, office-issued laptop (where admin rights block installing games on C: drive), or a shared family PC. Booting from an external SSD lets them game without touching or slowing down the main Windows installation.
2. Where Your Idea Will Fail (Be Realistic)
Hardcore PC Gamers Won't Buy It: Someone who already owns a ₹80,000 gaming laptop and knows how to tweak Windows settings will never pay ₹13,000 for this—they will just buy a bare SSD and install Steam themselves.
Empty Drive at ₹10k–₹13k is a Tough Sell: If a buyer opens the box, boots up XPlay, and sees an empty menu where they still have to buy and download every game themselves, they will feel cheated and demand a refund.
3. How to Make It Irresistible (Pivot Your Offer)
If you want high sales and low returns, adjust your value proposition slightly:
Bundle a Wireless Controller: Include a solid wireless gamepad (costs you ~₹1,000–₹1,200 in bulk) inside the ₹10,999 / ₹13,999 box. Now it truly feels like buying a complete console kit rather than an expensive pen drive.
Pre-Install Top Free-to-Play PC & Retro Games: Pre-download popular free PC titles (Counter-Strike 2, Rocket League, Fall Guys, Dota 2, Apex Legends) and legal indie/retro games onto the SSD before cloning! That way, the moment the buyer plugs it in, 20+ great games are already installed and playable offline/online without waiting hours for downloads.
Target the Right Buyer: Market it on Instagram Reels not to hardcore PC builders, but to hostel students, casual laptop owners, and 90s/2000s kids who want to connect their laptop to a TV and play GTA, FIFA/Football, Tekken, and classic PlayStation/PSP games with friends using controllers.
If you'd like to test this angle, I can help you map out a list of top free-to-play PC games + classic emulator setups that fit on a 500GB drive so buyers get instant value the second they boot it up. Would that help?




